          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "Thank you for sharing that. Here is what the educational reference says about coronary artery disease (source: \"Coronary artery disease,\" General clinical reference).\n\n- **What it is:** A build-up of fatty plaque in the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. It develops over many years.\n- **Two ways it causes symptoms:** Gradual narrowing can cause predictable chest discomfort during exertion, called stable angina. A plaque can also rupture and trigger a clot that suddenly blocks the artery, which is an acute coronary syndrome, such as a heart attack. This can happen even at a plaque that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms, so a normal past stress test does not rule out a future heart attack.\n\nThe passages I found did not cover treatment, medicines, or what to ask your clinician about your own situation. Your cardiologist or primary care clinician is the right person for those questions.\n\n**Seek emergency care right away** if you have chest pain or pressure that is severe, lasts more than a few minutes, or does not ease with rest, or if you have chest pain with shortness of breath, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back. Call your local emergency number. Do not wait to see if it passes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
