          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is meant by secondary prevention?",
          "answer": "Secondary prevention means the steps taken by someone who already has established coronary artery disease, such as a past heart attack, a procedure to open an artery, or artery narrowing seen on imaging. The goal is to lower the chance of another heart event. (Source: \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis,\" major cardiology society guidance, educational summary.)\n\nThe guidance describes it as a package rather than a single treatment. Its components include long-term antiplatelet therapy (medicines that reduce clotting), high-intensity statin therapy (a cholesterol-lowering medicine), and, according to the same guidance, non-drug measures. The guidance notes that when people miss out on care, it is often the non-drug parts that get dropped. The passages I retrieved were cut off before they described those non-drug measures in full, so I cannot list them accurately here.\n\nAnother passage from the General clinical reference (Coronary artery disease) lists risk factors that can be changed, including smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber. Age, sex, and family history cannot be changed.\n\nThe passages did not say how any of these measures should be applied to your own case. Please discuss your specific treatment plan with your cardiologist or primary care clinician.\n\n**If you have chest pain or pressure that is severe, lasts more than a few minutes, does not ease with rest, or comes with shortness of breath, sweating, or nausea, call your local emergency number right away.**\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
