          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which parts of that are lifestyle rather than medication?",
          "answer": "The earlier passages did not list the non-drug measures for coronary artery disease in full, so I can only partly answer this.\n\n**Medication (from the secondary prevention guidance):** long-term antiplatelet therapy (medicines that reduce clotting) and high-intensity statin therapy (a cholesterol-lowering medicine).\n\n**Lifestyle (from the General clinical reference, Coronary artery disease):** the changeable risk factors are smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber. Some of these are also treated with medicines, so lifestyle and medication often overlap.\n\nMy lifestyle search returned passages on activity and diet, but they were written for high blood pressure, type 2 diabetes, COPD, and heart failure, not coronary artery disease. Two points may be relevant to you:\n\n- **Activity (High blood pressure guidance):** people with known heart disease should be assessed before starting vigorous activity. If chest discomfort, severe breathlessness, or faintness happens during exercise, stop and seek assessment.\n- **Diet (High blood pressure guidance):** reducing alcohol and reading sodium on labels are described as useful non-drug steps. Potassium-based salt substitutes are not right for everyone, so check with your clinician before using one.\n\nPlease ask your cardiologist or primary care clinician which changes suit you and how to start them safely.\n\n**If you have chest pain or pressure that is severe, lasts more than a few minutes, does not ease with rest, or comes with shortness of breath, sweating, or nausea, call your local emergency number right away.**\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
